  <running_head>THEOD. BEZÆ</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
    <catchword>Et</catchword>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <p><i>Tristes per Umbras ambiguis secant.</i></p>
    <p><i>Quotquot resurgens Sol renouat dies</i></p>
    <p><i>Cursu rotantur dissimili, pigras</i></p>
    <p><i>Æstate ducunt frugifera moras:</i></p>
    <p><i>Bruma rigentis temporibus statim</i></p>
    <p><i>Volant recisi, mox niveo nitent</i></p>
    <p><i>Fulgore sudi, mox nigricantibus</i></p>
    <p><i>Velantur Umbris, mox rapido impigris</i></p>
    <p><i>Ardore messes agricolis coquunt,</i></p>
    <p><i>Mox cuncta inerti rura premunt gelu.</i></p>
    <p><i>Nunc terra odoro multiplices sinu</i></p>
    <p><i>Flores refundit, quum placidus novo</i></p>
    <p><i>Demulcet auras Vere Favonius,</i></p>
    <p><i>Latisque Vestit frondibus arbores.</i></p>
    <p><i>Nunc squallet omnis, quum Boreas plaga</i></p>
    <p><i>Frigus reducens Sarmatica ruit:</i></p>
    <p><i>Quum sæva campos pallidulis hyems</i></p>
    <p><i>Spargit pruinis, et nive candida.</i></p>
    <p><i>Nec Vultus idem caruleis manet</i></p>
    <p><i>Ubique ponti fluctibus: aut sua</i></p>
    <p><i>Feruente quassans littora murmure</i></p>
    <p><i>Urbes propinquas territat, et satis</i></p>
    <p><i>Minatur agris diluuiem: aut Noto</i></p>
    <p><i>Silente, et Eurus, atque Aquilonibus,</i></p>
    <p><i>Deponit iras littoribus suis</i></p>
    <p><i>Hærens quietus, nec Zephyro abnuit</i></p>
    <p><i>Leni pauentes tergore nautas.</i></p>
    <p><i>Non ille prudens qui dubiis Videt</i></p>
    <p><i>Volui perorbem motibus omnia,</i></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Show-through from the facing page at the right margin; the catchword "Et" is partly cut off. Some letters in the Greek-style names (Zephyro, Vere) are uncertain; the long s is transcribed as s throughout. Line-initial capital letters with a decorative form, such as "Ʃ" and "Ṽ", are read as S and V.</notes>
</page>
